WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.700
<v Narrator>So now let's talk about Trusted Advisor.</v>

2
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.700
So it's a service, you don't have to install anything,

3
00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:08.790
and it's going to give you a high level account assessment.

4
00:00:08.790 --> 00:00:10.200
So it's going to analyze your account

5
00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:13.110
and provide recommendations on several categories

6
00:00:13.110 --> 00:00:15.180
such as cost optimization,

7
00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:18.240
performance, security, fault tolerance,

8
00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:21.330
service limits and operational excellence,

9
00:00:21.330 --> 00:00:24.210
and I've put cost optimization, service limits,

10
00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:25.860
and operational excellence in bold

11
00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:28.680
because I think they are important going into the exam.

12
00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:31.410
So all customers of AWS have access

13
00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:34.050
to the core checks and recommendations.

14
00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:36.750
It's a few checks, but if you want to have access

15
00:00:36.750 --> 00:00:40.530
to the full trusted advisor, this is only available

16
00:00:40.530 --> 00:00:43.950
for business and enterprise support plans.

17
00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:45.660
On top of it, you can have weekly

18
00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:47.850
email notifications from the console

19
00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:51.300
directly set up from Trusted Advisor.

20
00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:54.180
Now, if you have the full Trusted Advisor,

21
00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:56.230
again, only available for enterprise

22
00:00:57.301 --> 00:00:59.460
and business support plans, then you have the ability

23
00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:02.400
to select CloudWatch alarms when reaching limits,

24
00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:04.800
and you get programmatic access

25
00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:07.683
using the AWS Support API.

26
00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:11.430
So we can talk about AWS support plans

27
00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:12.690
and there's four of them.

28
00:01:12.690 --> 00:01:16.530
There's basic support, developer, business and enterprise.

29
00:01:16.530 --> 00:01:18.780
Now, you don't need to remember the differences

30
00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:20.850
between all these support plans,

31
00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:22.260
but let's go over the basics

32
00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:24.270
that you do need to remember for the exam.

33
00:01:24.270 --> 00:01:26.280
The first one is basic support

34
00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:29.430
and it's included for all customers and it's free.

35
00:01:29.430 --> 00:01:30.810
And for Trusted Advisor,

36
00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:34.650
you're going to get seven score checks.

37
00:01:34.650 --> 00:01:36.150
If you use the developer plan,

38
00:01:37.326 --> 00:01:38.555
it's something you have to pay for.

39
00:01:38.555 --> 00:01:39.388
You get more business support from AWS,

40
00:01:39.388 --> 00:01:42.780
but it still only gives you access to seven core checks.

41
00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:44.610
As soon as you go into the business

42
00:01:44.610 --> 00:01:46.860
or the enterprise support plan,

43
00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:48.480
then you're going to have access

44
00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:51.570
to the full set of checks from Trusted Advisor

45
00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:52.860
and you're going to get

46
00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:56.040
programmatic access to the Trusted Advisor

47
00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:58.470
through the AWS support API.

48
00:01:58.470 --> 00:02:00.180
And that is something you have to remember

49
00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:01.590
going into the exam.

50
00:02:01.590 --> 00:02:03.690
So here are a few things you should know

51
00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:05.310
about Trusted Advisor.

52
00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:06.840
First of all, Trusted Advisor has a check

53
00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:09.480
to know if your S3 buckets are public,

54
00:02:09.480 --> 00:02:11.910
but it cannot check whether there are objects

55
00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:14.490
within your bucket that are made public,

56
00:02:14.490 --> 00:02:16.770
so therefore it's not the right solution for that.

57
00:02:16.770 --> 00:02:19.830
You should use Amazon Event Bridge or S3 Events

58
00:02:19.830 --> 00:02:24.330
or create an AWS config rule to check for compliance.

59
00:02:24.330 --> 00:02:25.680
In terms of service limits, yes,

60
00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:29.220
you can monitor service limits in Trusted Advisor,

61
00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:31.440
but if you want to increase a limit, you would need

62
00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:34.410
to manually open a support center case

63
00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:37.500
to increase its limits, or you can use the new API

64
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:39.987
of the AWS service quota's service.

65
00:02:39.987 --> 00:02:42.660
So that means that you can come up

66
00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:44.280
with some very funky architecture

67
00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:46.350
to monitor your limits at scale,

68
00:02:46.350 --> 00:02:47.850
which I'm just showing you here,

69
00:02:47.850 --> 00:02:49.710
and you can have a look at it right now.

70
00:02:49.710 --> 00:02:53.700
So here, for example, we have a Lambda function

71
00:02:53.700 --> 00:02:56.640
that is going to refresh Trusted Advisor once in a while,

72
00:02:56.640 --> 00:02:58.290
and then Trusted Advisor has checks,

73
00:02:58.290 --> 00:03:02.010
which is going to evoke an event bridge rule.

74
00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:04.290
We can do the same with service quotas.

75
00:03:04.290 --> 00:03:07.260
So this time we can check for VCPUs in service quotas.

76
00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:10.590
This is a lower kind of limit you can have in that service.

77
00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:14.580
And again, in case something is being like over the limit

78
00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:17.970
or at the limit, then we can evoke an event rule.

79
00:03:17.970 --> 00:03:19.590
We can do this cross account as well.

80
00:03:19.590 --> 00:03:21.390
So the same kind of architecture

81
00:03:21.390 --> 00:03:23.280
works in a secondary account.

82
00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:25.110
And then thanks to event buses,

83
00:03:25.110 --> 00:03:28.320
we can have the events go from one EventBus

84
00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:30.810
to the central primary EventBus.

85
00:03:30.810 --> 00:03:33.870
From there, EventBridge may just have multiple targets.

86
00:03:33.870 --> 00:03:37.099
For example, an SNS topic to receive emails as an admin

87
00:03:37.099 --> 00:03:40.260
or an SQSQ so that you have a Lambda function

88
00:03:40.260 --> 00:03:42.720
that automatically summarizes its limits

89
00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:45.210
and sends them into a DynamoDB table

90
00:03:45.210 --> 00:03:47.610
and maybe a DLQ in case these events

91
00:03:47.610 --> 00:03:49.500
cannot be processed correctly.

92
00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:52.230
And finally, a Slack notifier

93
00:03:52.230 --> 00:03:54.720
to notify your admins that they're on Slack.

94
00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:57.900
That's something is happening in terms of your limits.

95
00:03:57.900 --> 00:03:58.950
And you need to have your admins

96
00:03:58.950 --> 00:04:01.380
have a look at it right now and Parameter Store

97
00:04:01.380 --> 00:04:03.420
if you wanted to get some credential informations

98
00:04:03.420 --> 00:04:05.520
from Slack stored securely.

99
00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:07.110
So I really like this architecture,

100
00:04:07.110 --> 00:04:08.910
and you can even improve it if you wanted

101
00:04:08.910 --> 00:04:12.000
to have automatically limits increases

102
00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:13.560
by making this Lambda function here

103
00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:17.070
actually increase the limits through the service quotas API,

104
00:04:17.070 --> 00:04:18.750
but this is a cool architecture.

105
00:04:18.750 --> 00:04:20.760
So that's it for Trusted Advisor.

106
00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:23.913
I hope you liked it, and I will see you in the next lecture.

